---
import DocsLayout from "../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="NVIDIA graphics">
    <h1>NVIDIA graphics</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Octlitch does not include proprietary NVIDIA drivers in the
        installation image. They can be installed after Octlitch is
        installed.
    </p>

    <h2>Before installing</h2>

    <p>
        The Octlitch live environment and initial installation use the
        graphics support available in the Fedora base.
    </p>

    <p>
        After installation, users with supported NVIDIA hardware can choose
        to install the proprietary NVIDIA driver.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Do not use NVIDIA's .run installer</strong>

        <p>
            Octlitch uses RPM packages so graphics drivers stay integrated
            with the Fedora package manager and kernel update process.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Check your graphics hardware</h2>

    <p>
        Display the detected graphics adapters and their current kernel
        drivers:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <h2>Install the NVIDIA driver</h2>

    <p>
        Octlitch includes a post-install helper for installing NVIDIA
        support:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo octlitch-install-nvidia</code></pre>

    <p>
        The helper configures the required third-party repositories,
        installs the NVIDIA driver packages, builds the kernel module and
        updates the initial RAM filesystem where required.
    </p>

    <p>
        Follow the instructions shown in the terminal and allow the process
        to finish before restarting the computer.
    </p>

    <h2>Restart the system</h2>

    <p>
        After the installation completes:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo reboot</code></pre>

    <h2>Verify the driver</h2>

    <p>
        After restarting, check that the NVIDIA driver is available:
    </p>

    <pre><code>nvidia-smi</code></pre>

    <p>
        A working installation should display information about the NVIDIA
        GPU and installed driver.
    </p>

    <p>
        You can also check the active kernel driver with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <h2>Secure Boot</h2>

    <p>
        Secure Boot can prevent third-party kernel modules from loading
        unless they are trusted by the system.
    </p>

    <p>
        Check whether Secure Boot is enabled with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>mokutil --sb-state</code></pre>

    <p>
        If Secure Boot is enabled, the NVIDIA installation may require
        additional module-signing or key-enrollment steps.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Read the installer output</strong>

        <p>
            The Octlitch NVIDIA helper explains the Secure Boot situation
            when it detects that additional steps may be necessary.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Kernel updates</h2>

    <p>
        Octlitch uses the RPM Fusion akmod packaging method for the
        proprietary NVIDIA module.
    </p>

    <p>
        After a kernel update, the NVIDIA module may need a short period
        to build for the new kernel before the system is restarted.
    </p>

    <p>
        You can check the build state with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo akmods --force</code></pre>

    <h2>If the NVIDIA driver does not load</h2>

    <p>
        Start by checking the installed packages and loaded kernel modules:
    </p>

    <pre><code>rpm -qa | grep -i nvidia</code></pre>

    <pre><code>lsmod | grep nvidia</code></pre>

    <p>
        Check kernel messages related to NVIDIA:
    </p>

    <pre><code>journalctl -b -k | grep -i nvidia</code></pre>

    <p>
        Also verify Secure Boot status:
    </p>

    <pre><code>mokutil --sb-state</code></pre>

    <h2>Laptops with multiple GPUs</h2>

    <p>
        Some laptops contain both integrated graphics and a discrete NVIDIA
        GPU. In these systems, applications may use different GPUs depending
        on the hardware and driver configuration.
    </p>

    <p>
        Both graphics devices can be inspected with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>lspci -k | grep -EA3 'VGA|3D|Display'</code></pre>

    <div class="docs-next">
        <a href="/documentation/hardware/graphics/">
            Next: AMD and Intel graphics →
        </a>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
